<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Porto-Novo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Porto-Novo is a port city on the coast of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, now its official capital. In the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries it was an outlet for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>. Captives taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>'s cavalry were sold to European traders there and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouidah">Ouidah</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/porto-novo.jpg" alt="Porto-Novo" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: GBETONGNINOUGBO JOSEPH HERVE AHISSOU · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grande_Mosquee_Porto-Novo_Benin_Joseph_Herve_Ahissou.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Glele (r. 1858–1889) the kingdom faced growing French influence at <b>Porto-Novo</b> and disputes over Cotonou, which Dahomey had ceded in treaties of uncertain validity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Oyo was then at the height of its power, with a tributary network that extended from the Niger in the east across the Aja lands to the west and included Dahomey, Allada and <b>Porto-Novo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>That year the colony of Dahomey and dependencies was formed, with <b>Porto-Novo</b> as its capital, and French forces extended it north to the Niger.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-dahomey">French Dahomey</a></li><li><blockquote>Portuguese traders named the place <b>Porto-Novo</b>, and the kingdom grew as a port for the Atlantic trade, often under pressure from the Oyo Empire and later the Kingdom of Dahomey.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-porto-novo">Kingdom of Porto-Novo</a></li></ul></section>
